  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We adapt a multi-fidelity optimization framework combining derivative-free optimization with hybrid repair mechanisms. Unlike the parent algorithm that relies on gradient estimation and smoothing, our approach leverages delayed constraint enforcement and surrogate objectives to balance between exploration and exploitation. The core idea is to transform the optimization landscape by approximating the discontinuous penalty with a barrier function that separates the feasible and infeasible regions. This adaptation specifically targets the high-dimensional coupling between overlap terms at different lags using adaptive differentiation. The method reduces the risk of premature convergence by maintaining constraint satisfaction during exploration phases.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Constraint Enforcement: Formulate problem with explicit bounds and integral constraint. Key objective term: max_{k} corr(h(x), 1-h(x+k)).\n   - Repair Mechanism: Minimize violations in repair steps rather than fixing them: minimize (sum(h) - n/2)^2 subject to clipped values. This promotes structure preservation.\n   - Hybrid Representation: Combine cluster-based and gradient-guided phases with M-steps. Each hyperparameter is optimized locally using projective geometry to maintain constraint closure.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Step-by-step:\n   a) Pre-initialize with provided initial samples (n=560)\n   a1) Conduct phase-separation with n_initial = 560\n   a2) Apply adaptive triangle-cutting to maintain convex components\n   \n   b) Core optimization cycle:\n   b1) Descend through projected gradients\n   b2) Maintain repair potential via adaptive gradient splicing\n   b3) Cluster-based restart every 300 seconds\n   b4) Apply multidirectional mutation to active constraints\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Default configuration:\n   - M-steps dynamic adjustment: \u03c3_start=0.2, \u03c3_final=0.8\n   - Repair frequency: triggered by 5 step default deviation\n   - Triangular decomposition bi-weekly restart\n\n   Alternate strategies include:\n   A) Gradient explosion control\n   B) Random projection dimensionality reduction\n   C) Saddle-shaped constraint enrichment\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   Total wall-clock:\n   - FPOOL: 400 seconds\n   - REPAIR: 200 seconds cumulative\n   - CLUSTERING: 300 seconds\n   - GAUSSIAN: 200 seconds\n\n   Checkpoints inserted at 1/6th monitoring intervals. Repair threshold: ||\u2207f|| < 1e-4 after 10 steps.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Final post-processing:\n   - Re-evaluate objective on repaired candidate\n   - Check constraint satisfaction: ||sum(h) - 280|| < 1e-4\n   - Ensure Lipschitz continuity (||f||_L < 1e-3)\n   \n   Return validity signature: (feasible, objective, n_valid)\n   with strict: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and integral=280 with tolerance 1e-4.\n   \n   Contracts: \n   - Repair steps occur every 30 iterations\n   - Objective evaluation every 5 iterations\n   \n   The method's stopping condition is either 1100 seconds or 100 functional calls.",
